senses.gloss:
cailaka,
(1) m. (from Skt. cela or caila, cloth), a monk (cf. Burnouf, Intr., 〔57〕; Lotus, 〔392〕; Kern, SBE 〔21.186 note 1〕), said to mean, specifically, one who wears no more than a (loin-)cloth: te cailaka-bhūta SP 〔192.9〕 (vs), turned into monks; but most mss. read celaka, ceḍaka, or cellaka, or the like;
(2) nt. (= Skt. cela, caila, also celaka, see Schmidt, Nachträge; AMg. celaa), cloth: ˚kaṃ, acc. sg., Mv 〔iii.163.9〕 (prose).
